Z||000000||Rex||05-09-2001||06:37 PM||||Anyone have a pic of what this type of "scoop" would look like on a notchback.No thanks I would'nt care for any rice. Also how would the hatch open? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||05-09-2001||06:48 PM||||Are you talking about the over-the-roof" type of scoops? Like the Indy scoop? I have one on my car. There are 2 ways to mount them. One is to bolt it up and it has to be unbolted to open the back and the second is a hinged system. Just undo the latch and the scoop hinges back so the decklid can be opened. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/All_pics_1.jpg"> <P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rex||05-09-2001||06:59 PM||||Thanks Oreif thats it. How is your mounted? Where can they be purchased? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||05-09-2001||09:16 PM||||Mine is bolted on. So you have to remove the 4 bolts (Allen screws) to open the decklid. The reason I did it was I wanted it unrestricted. With the latch/hinge system the inside is blocked 1/2 way and I have heard that if you don't pin the latch it could pop loose then the scoop would rip off the car if it was moving at highway speeds.<BR>I got mine from IRM. Although they say it is "non-functional" you can make it functional by cutting out the face and a hole on the decklid. If you do a search for "indy scoop" with my name, You'll see the other posts where I have pics of how it is mounted and a few other places that sell them.<P><BR>Go to this thread for pics and more detailed info.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/004896.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/004896.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-09-2001).]||||reg||1
